/Pg 17 0 R
/K [26]
>>
endobj
301 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 148 0 R
/E (Every under-buffered redemption pushes two RedemptionRequest structs onto an ever- growing array. nextQueueIndex only advances; completed entries are never deleted or compacted. Over a long lifetime the array can become very large, increasing gas cost of any future operation that reads length and contributing to permanent storage bloat on the chain.)
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [27]
>>
endobj
302 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 148 0 R
/E (Response By Team This issue has been acknowledged by the team and a fall back Pyrh oracla has been implemented.)
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [28]
>>
endobj
303 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 148 0 R
/E (All pricing depends on one AggregatorV3Interface. There is no second feed, TWAP, or maximum-deviation check against a previous price. A compromised, frozen, or grossly erroneous feed \(within the staleness window\) can cause mis-minting or mis-redemption.)
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [29]
>>
endobj
304 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 148 0 R
/E (Impact: Economic loss or unfair advantage if the oracle reports a wrong but "fresh" price.)
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [30]
>>
endobj
305 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 148 0 R
/E (Remediation It is recommended to consider a dual-oracle or Chainlink + alternative feed with a relative deviation circuit-breaker. Document the chosen feed's update frequency and the rationale for the chosen staleness threshold.)
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [31]
>>
endobj
